<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.3: The <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>- and <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercepts of Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01907</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.3: The X- and Y-Intercepts of Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>55abafa1-c8ec-464f-ab9b-4109464f7f1c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Here are pairs of expressions in standard and factored forms. Each pair of expressions define the same quadratic function, which can be represented with the given graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(y\)-intercept of each graph.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Function \(f\)
    \(x^2+4x+3\)
    \((x+3)(x+1)\)
    
    (<media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_01.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Graph of \(f\): \(x\)-intercepts: \((-3,0)\) and \((-1,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_67f58a73-d9ba-4806-a5a0-801efa0f1623" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            
            <para id="para-00006">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Correct! Graph of \(f\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,3)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is 3. Graph of \(f\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,3)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>Function \(g\)
    \(x^2-5x+4\)
    \((x-4)(x-1)\)
    
    (<media alt="Graph of nonlinear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_02.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Graph of \(g\): \(x\)-intercepts: \((1,0)\) and \((4,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_178b12d7-ad7f-4a82-91b9-f32055d91df2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00012">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00013">Correct! Graph of \(g\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,4)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is 4. Graph of \(g\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,4)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
        <item>Function \(h\)
    \(x^2-9\)
    \((x-3)(x+3)\)
    
    (<media alt="Graph of nonlinear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_03.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">Graph of \(h\): \(x\)-intercepts: \((3,0)\) and \((-3,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_561a8641-ac55-4e73-b668-3e53f80fb1f4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            
            <para id="para-00018">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct!– 9, Graph of \(h\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,-9)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00020">The correct answer is -9. Graph of \(h\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,-9)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
        <item>Function \(j\)
    \(x^2-5x\)
    \( x(x-5)\)
    
    (<media alt="Graph of non linear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_04.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00021">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Graph of \(j\): \(x\)-intercepts: \((0,0)\) and \((5,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_3725009b-5c7a-484f-9d88-30de64a4796e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            
            <para id="para-00024">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00025">Correct! 0, Graph of \(j\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is 0, Graph of \(j\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00005">
        <item>Function \(k\)
    \(5x-x^2\)
    \(x(5-x)\)
    
    (<media alt="Graph of nonlinear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_05.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00027">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Graph of \(k\): \(x\)-intercepts: \((0,0)\) and \((5,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="_890a1cc8-6178-4361-910f-b9f1fd5ef70d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            
            <para id="para-00030">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00031">Correct! 0, Graph of \(k\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00032">The correct answer is 0, Graph of \(k\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
        <item>Function \(l\)
    \(x^2+4x+4\)
    \((x+2)(x+2)\)
    
    (<media alt="Graph of non linear function."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_06_06.svg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
        <exercise id="_91e3b870-5ded-41d7-b8b2-0d67ab29631a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            
            <para id="para-00033">Identify the \(x\)-intercept of the graph. 
            
           <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00034">Correct! – 2, Graph of \(l\): \(x\)-intercept \((-2,0)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00035">The correct answer is – 2. Graph of \(l\): \(x\)-intercept \((-2,0)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d8582297-86cb-47b6-9ce1-c1e401c54266" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            
            <para id="para-00036">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00037">Correct! Graph of \(l\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,4)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00038">The correct answer is 4. Graph of \(l\): \(y\)-intercept \((0,4)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00039">What do you notice about the \(x\)-intercepts, the \(y\)-intercept, and the numbers in the expressions defining each function? Make a couple of observations.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00041">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <list id="list-00007">
              <item>The \(y\)-coordinate of the \(y\)-intercept is the constant term when the function is expressed in standard form. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,-9)\) for the function \(h\), \(x^2-9\).</item>
              <item>The \(x\)-intercepts in each graph seem to match the numbers in the factors of the expressions in factored form, but the signs seem to be the opposite. For example, when the expression is \((x+3)(x+1)\), the \(x\)-intercepts are \((-3,0)\) and \((-1,0)\).</item>
              <item>When one of the factors is just a variable (rather than a sum or a difference), one of the \(x\)-intercepts is \((0,0)\). For the function \(i\), \(x(x-5)\), one of the intercepts is \((0,0)\)).</item>
              <item>x(x-5) and x(5-x) have the same \(x\)-intercepts, but one graph opens upward and the other opens downward. </item>
              <item>When the two factors are the same, there is only one \(x\)-intercept. For the function \(l\), \((x+2)(x+2)\), – 2 is the \(x\)-intercept.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00008">
        <item>Here is an expression that models function \(p\), another quadratic function: \((x-9)(x-1)\).</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <para id="para-00042">Predict the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents this function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00044">The \(x\)-intercepts will be \((9,0)\) and \((1,0)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
        <exercise id="c7f9f4d6-6cff-4424-897a-2c5368a6ce76" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            
            <para id="para-00045">Identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph that represents this function.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00046">Correct! The \(y\)-intercept will be \((0,9)\) because 9 is the constant term when the expression is put in standard form.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The correct answer is 9. The \(y\)-intercept will be \((0,9)\) because 9 is the constant term when the expression is put in standard form.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
